<h1><span lang="EN-GB">Average Noise Reduction (Scan Data)</span></h1>
<hr align="left" width="98.974%" size="6" class="whs3">
<p>This command is accessible from <span class="Path" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Scan Data &gt; Reduce Noise &gt; Average</b></span>.</p>
<p class="Description"><b><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Description:</b></span></b> <span class="Command">Average Noise Reduction</span> <span lang="EN-GB">command reduces noise in the visible selected scan data by applying an average (Laplacian) filter. Co-ordinates of every point are based on the average of co-ordinates of its neighbouring points. Local curvature is also taking into account  see</span> <u><span lang="EN-GB"><a href="Point_Curvature.htm">Point Curvature</a></span></u> <span lang="EN-GB">for details. Calibrated value for the local curvature is between 0 (peek) and 1 (flat) and it is multiplied to the point offset that must be made. This leads to maximum offset (maximum noise reduction) on flat areas and very slight offset on curved areas.</span></p>
<p><span lang="EN-GB">This command is grayed if there is no selection or all selections are hidden. It works on wholly selected entities as well as on partial selection.</span></p>
<p><span lang="EN-GB">All points in the data will be modified, including critical features such as edges (in lesser degree). Using a low strength setting will mean that only small changes will be made to the data. Increasing the strength setting will result in smoother data with larger changes.</span></p>
<p><img src="../images/image40.jpg" width="321px" height="199px" border="0" class="img_whs4" alt=""></p>
<p class="parameterstitle"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Strength</b></span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-GB">Specifies how strong the filter to be applied, i.e. number of iterations. It ranges from 1 to 40.</span> <span lang="EN-GB" style="margin-left:19px;">Default strength for this command is 1.</span></p>
